course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 21
title: Bruises and hand-washing; cats, dogs and ponies
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: You trip in the park and land on your kolIno, your knee: "ya vpAv!" — I fell over — and Sashko asks "vse dobre?", are you okay?
explain: Next day there's a sinYak, a bruise, and it is named after its colour: syniy means blue, so a bruise is "the blue one".
explain: Before every meal Sashko's mum says "pomyi ruky!", wash your hands — every single time.
explain: Then the pets: a cat is kit (a tomcat) or kIshka (a she-cat), a cuddly kitty is kotyk; a dog is pes or sobAka, a puppy tsutsyk; a horse is kIn and a pony kOnyk.
explain: Grown-ups use some of these as loving names: "kotyku!" means "darling!", and a little child may be called "serdenko", little heart.
fact: "kolIno" means knee; "rozbyv kolIno" means "I scraped my knee", and to kneel is "staty na kolina".
fact: "SErtse" means heart; "serdenko", little heart, is a loving word for a small child, like "sweetheart".
fact: "plechE" means shoulder.
fact: "lytsE" means face, and "pomyi lytsE" means "wash your face".
fact: "ya vpAv" means "I fell over" when a boy says it; a girl says "ya vpala", and the kind answer is "vse dobre?", are you okay?
fact: "sinYak" means a bruise; it comes from syniy, blue, because of the bruise's colour.
fact: "pomyi ruky" means "wash your hands", and the friend's mum will say it before every meal.
fact: "kit" means a cat or tomcat and sounds like the English word "kit"; "kIshka" means a she-cat or any cat.
fact: "kotyk" means kitty or pussycat, "kotyky" means kitties, and "kotyku!" is also a cuddly word for a person, like "darling".
fact: "pes" and "sobAka" both mean dog; "pes" sounds like "pess" and is not the English word "pest".
fact: "tsutsyk" means puppy or doggie and is a loving word.
fact: "kIn" means horse, and "kOnyk" means pony or little horse.
exercise: Sashko comes in with a scraped "kolIno". What did he hurt? | His knee | His elbow | His chin | His ankle
exercise: What does "SErtse" mean? | Heart | Head | Stomach | Shoulder
exercise: Sashko's granny calls his baby sister "serdenko". What is she calling her? | Sweetheart | Little monster | Little piglet | Little thief
exercise: Sashko carries his little brother on his "plechE". Where is he? | On his shoulder | Riding on his back | On his knee | Up on his head
exercise: Sashko's mum says "pomyi lytsE". What must he wash? | His face | His hands | His feet | His hair
exercise: Sashko trips and calls "ya vpAv!". What happened? | He fell over | He won the race | He's hungry | He got lost
exercise: Your friend falls off the swing. What do you ask? | vse dobre? | yak teBE zvut? | SKILky tobI rokIv? | shcho NOvoho?
exercise: How does a girl say "I fell over"? | ya vpala | ya vpAv | ya vpAvla | ya pAla
exercise: Why is a bruise called "sinYak"? | From syniy, blue, its colour | From "sin", a bad deed | From the sound of a fall | From a word for "soft"
exercise: Right before dinner, what will Sashko's mum always say? | pomyi ruky | DOdomu! | ne bIisya | odYahnysya!
exercise: What is the difference between "kit" and "kIshka"? | A tomcat and a she-cat | A cat and a kitten | A cat and a dog | A wild cat and a pet cat
exercise: Sashko's sister strokes the cat: "kotyk!". What is she saying? | Kitty! | Bad cat! | Dog! | Go away!
exercise: Sashko's mum calls his dad "kotyku". What does it mean here? | Darling | Lazy cat | Silly | Hurry up
exercise: What are "pes" and "sobAka"? | Two words for dog | A dog and a puppy | A dog and a wolf | A cat and a dog
exercise: Sashko's neighbour has a new "tsutsyk". What is it? | A puppy | A kitten | A hamster | A pony
exercise: At the farm Sashko points: "kIn!" and then at a small one: "kOnyk!". What does he show you? | A horse and a pony | A cow and a calf | A dog and a puppy | A goat and a kid
test: my mate says "pes" means pest, so Ukrainians call dogs pests. right? | No, pes simply means dog | Yes, it means pest or nuisance | Yes, but only for naughty dogs | No, it's the word for a cat
test: Sashko's mum calls him "kotyku" in front of you and he goes red. Is she calling him a cat? | No, it's a loving word, like "darling" | Yes, she thinks he's furry | Yes, she is telling him off for being lazy | No, it means "hurry up" in Ukrainian
test: Sashko says "ya vpAv" and your mate laughs. What is the kinder answer? | vse dobre? | smIshno! | ty proHRAv | molodEts!
test: Your mate thinks "sinYak" comes from "sin", like a punishment. Where does it really come from? | syniy, the colour blue | A punishment word | A word for falling | A word for doctor
test: You touch the bread before dinner without washing. What will Sashko's mum most likely say? | pomyi ruky! | pomyi lytsE! | ne bIisya! | DOdomu!
test: In a story, the hero is told to "staty na kolina" before the king. What must he do? | Kneel | Run away | Sit on a chair | Shake hands
test: why is "kit" funny for English kids? | It sounds like the English word "kit" | It sounds like "kite" and means a kite | It is a rude word | It means "kid"
test: Sashko's cousin calls the puppy "tsutsyk" and strokes it. Is it a telling-off word? | No, it's a loving word for a puppy | Yes, it means "bad dog" when you scold | Yes, it's what you shout for "go away" | No, it's the word for a little kitten
test: A she-cat walks in with kittens. Which word fits the mother best? | kIshka | kit | pes | kOnyk
test: What would happen if you asked for a "kIn" at the pet shop? | They'd laugh: kIn is a horse | They'd give you a little goldfish in a bag | They'd give you a kitten | They'd give you a puppy
test: Sashko's dad lifts him on his "plechE" to see the parade. Where is Sashko? | On his dad's shoulders | On his dad's knee | In his dad's arms | On his dad's back
exam: Identify the etymological basis of the Ukrainian noun "sinYak", denoting a bruise. | The adjective syniy, signifying blue, after the colour of the contusion | The noun sin, signifying a transgression | A verb signifying to fall | A noun signifying a physician
exam: It is given that a hostess pronounces "pomyi ruky" prior to a meal. Determine the action required of the guest. | The washing of the hands | The washing of the face | The laying of the table | The removal of the shoes
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression "kotyku" when addressed by one adult to another? | It is a term of endearment, comparable to "darling" | It is a reproach for idleness, likening the addressee to a lazy cat | It is an instruction to make haste | It is a reference to a domestic animal only
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the nouns "kIn" and "kOnyk". | The former denotes a horse and the latter a pony | The former denotes a pony and the latter a horse | Both denote a dog | Both denote a cat
